🟦 FULL EXPLANATION (Every Line)
🔹 1. Debounce Function
function debounce(func, delay) {


func → the function you want to delay (e.g., searchAPI)

delay → how long to wait after typing stops (500 ms, etc.)

🔹 let timer;

A variable to store the timer ID.

🔹 Returned function
return function (...args) {


The returned function is what will actually run on the event.

...args means: take all arguments passed to this function.

If we do:

debouncedSearch("apple")


Then args = ["apple"]

🔹 clearTimeout(timer);

This is the most important part.

Every time the user presses a key:

previous timer is cancelled

new timer is started

This ensures the function runs only after typing stops.

🔹 Start a new timer
timer = setTimeout(() => {
    func.apply(this, args);
}, delay);


setTimeout runs func after delay milliseconds

.apply(this, args) ensures:

this value stays correct

arguments like "apple" are passed correctly

If the user keeps typing, the timer keeps resetting → function never executes.

Only when the user STOPS for 500ms, the function runs.

🟦 2. The actual function we want to debounce
function searchAPI(query) {
    console.log("Calling server for:", query);
}


This simulates an API call.

Before debounce → gets called many times
After debounce → called once

🟦 3. Using the Debounced Function
const debouncedSearch = debounce(searchAPI, 500);


We create a new function:

debouncedSearch()


This function:

waits 500ms after last input

then calls searchAPI

Then we attach it to the input event:

document.getElementById("search")
    .addEventListener("input", function () {
        debouncedSearch(this.value);
    });

🎉 WHAT WILL HAPPEN IN PRACTICE?

User types:

A P P L E


Browser fires INPUT event 5 times → but debounce cancels the previous timer each time.

Once user stops typing for 500ms, only then:

Calling server for: apple

